[B]Folia 26.1[/B]

(only EzRTP also completed the Folia run; the other tested plugins had compatibility or performance issues there)

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Plugin[/TH][TH]TP/s[/TH][TH]CPU / TP (ms)[/TH][TH]Watchdog stalls[/TH][TH]Success[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD][B]LeafRTP[/B][/TD][TD][B]12.5[/B][/TD][TD][B]4.15[/B][/TD][TD][B]0[/B][/TD][TD][B]100 %[/B][/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]EzRTP[/TD][TD]5.3[/TD][TD]6.34[/TD][TD]7 (one region 20.4 s)[/TD][TD]96.2 %[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

EzRTP's 7 watchdog stalls (one region unresponsive 20.4 s) are the server's own record of synchronous [I]World.loadChunk[/I] calls on region threads; LeafRTP issued none. For reference, the Pro Folia adapter cleared the same run at 13.5 TP/s. The shared [I]rtp-core[/I] engine, not a Pro-only adapter, carries the free build's Folia result.

[B]Caveats.[/B] Small client counts (2 on Paper, 3 on Folia); the Folia run's EzRTP failure is corroborated by the server's own watchdog log, independent of the harness. Competitor plugins update frequently; corrections welcome via GitHub issue with a contradicting repro or doc link.

Full methodology, raw CSVs, per-run analyses: [URL='https://github.com/dailystruggle/RTP/tree/V3/helpers/StressTestRTP'][I]helpers/StressTestRTP/[/I][/URL]. Video benchmark of [I]/rtp[/I] on a custom world generator: [URL='https://youtu.be/V0NyNK9JydM']youtu.be/V0NyNK9JydM[/URL].

[/SPOILER]


[SPOILER="Commands, placeholders, soft-deps"]

[B]Commands[/B] (full reference: [URL='https://dailystruggle.github.io/RTP/FOR_SERVER_ADMINS/']admin guide[/URL])

[LIST]
[*][B]/rtp[/B] - teleport to the default region for your current world or open gui (depending on addons).
[*][B]/rtp <parameter>=<value>[/B] - specify [I]region=[/I], [I]world=[/I], [I]player=[/I], or temporary overrides.
[*][B]/rtp reload[/B] - reload all configuration from disk.
[*][B]/rtp scan start|pause|resume|reset|cancel[/B] - build spatial memory by walking a region (renamed from [I]/rtp fill[/I] in 2.x). Demo: [URL='https://youtu.be/Ftjy1zw_S04']youtu.be/Ftjy1zw_S04[/URL].
[*][B]/rtp menu[/B] - interactive book menu.
[/LIST]

[B]PlaceholderAPI[/B]

[LIST]
[*][B]%rtp_player_status%[/B] - idle, waiting, teleporting, ...
[*][B]%rtp_total_queue_length%[/B], [B]%rtp_public_queue_length%[/B], [B]%rtp_personal_queue_length%[/B]
[*][B]%rtp_teleport_world%[/B], [B]%rtp_teleport_x%[/B], [B]%rtp_teleport_y%[/B], [B]%rtp_teleport_z%[/B]
[/LIST]

[B]Soft dependencies (all optional):[/B] Vault (for the optional economy charge), PlaceholderAPI, ProtocolLib. [I]PaperLib is no longer required.[/I]

[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER="FAQ"]

[B]Q: How do I stop /rtp from lagging my server, and why is LeafRTP faster than other random teleport plugins?[/B]
A: Most [I]/rtp[/I] calls serve from a pre-warmed queue - chunks are already loaded and safety-checked before you type the command. Two design choices make that queue cheap to keep full: a [B]persistent spatial memory[/B] per region (the plugin remembers which sectors of the world failed safety checks, so the spiral selector skips known-bad ground instead of rerolling forever), and an [B]off-tick async pre-filter[/B] (Anvil region files are read directly to reject unsafe biomes/blocks [I]before[/I] any chunk is loaded, so candidate verification never blocks the main thread). The pre-warmed queue is just the visible tip - the spatial memory keeps candidate selection bounded, and the async pre-filter keeps verification off the tick loop.

[B]Q: Is LeafRTP complicated to set up, and does it have economy, a GUI, and particle effects?[/B]
A: No, and yes. Drop the jar in and [I]/rtp[/I] works immediately, on a [I]default[/I] region written for you. Size that region to your world - shape, radius, center - and [I]/rtp menu[/I] does it in-game without editing YAML. It includes a clickable GUI menu, Vault economy (charge per teleport with per-region pricing), a particle / sound / firework effects engine, live map heatmaps, and twelve claim-plugin integrations.

[B]Q: Why is it called "LeafRTP" now instead of just "RTP"?[/B]
A: "RTP" is the generic term for random teleport, so the old name was nearly impossible to find - it collided with every other random-teleport plugin, command, and forum thread in search and marketplace indexes. "LeafRTP" is a distinct, indexable name that points unambiguously at this plugin while keeping the [I]/rtp[/I] command, [I]rtp-api[/I], config paths, and data files exactly as they were. Nothing changes for existing installs - only the public name.

[B]Q: Does it work with Iris / Terra / custom datapack generators?[/B]
A: Yes. Region files are read directly, so modded and namespaced biome and block IDs are preserved. No configuration needed. Because biome data comes from the populated [I].mca[/I] files rather than the live generator/noise-map lookup, [I]/rtp biome=<name>[/I] stays correct even on worlds that were pregenerated elsewhere or migrated across a Minecraft version, where seed-based biome assignment has drifted.

[B]Q: Do you support triangle / diamond region shapes?[/B]
A: Use the [I]Polygon[/I] shape - a triangle is a 3-vertex polygon and a diamond is a rotated square, so both are already expressible without a separate shape type.

[B]Q: Do I need Chunky or another pre-generator?[/B]
A: No, but they work well together. [I]/rtp scan[/I] walks a region off-tick, verifies safety, and generates any chunks it reaches that aren't on disk yet (via the server's own world generator) while recording which sectors are unsafe in persistent spatial memory. A separate pre-generator stays optional: run Chunky first if you want the whole map on disk up front, and scan will then read those chunks cheaply through the Anvil pre-filter instead of generating them as it goes.

[B]Q: I'm on NeoForge.[/B]
A: NeoForge is a first-class supported platform on Minecraft 1.21.x / 26.x - just drop the mod in.

[B]Q: I'm on Forge.[/B]
A: Run [B]Arclight[/B] or [B]Mohist[/B] (officially supported) and use this jar. A native Forge adapter is not planned.

[B]Q: Memory and MSPT - should I worry?[/B]
A: MSPT, no. Memory, know what you are buying: LeafRTP trades heap for tick time, on purpose. It is a cache, so it does [I]not[/I] use less RAM than a plugin that rerolls and keeps nothing. What it does do is stay bounded: every cache tier is capacity-limited, and the per-entry cost is small and measured against the current code - about 68 bytes per cached location in the hot buffers ([I]cacheCap[/I]), and about 26 bytes per known-bad chunk in the compressed 1D spatial segments, so 8 192 cached locations cost well under a megabyte. An active [I]MemoryTracker[/I] sweep prevents ticket leaks and chunk bloat, and background chunk generation pauses automatically under heap pressure ([I]maxHeapPercent[/I]). On a healthy server TPS holds near 20.00. If you are tight on RAM, lower [I]cacheCap[/I] - you trade queue depth for heap directly. I am deliberately not publishing a cross-plugin heap comparison: my last whole-JVM heap run predates the current allocation and cache-expiry work here, and competitor versions have moved on too, so any figure from it would describe neither this plugin nor the current version of anyone else's.

[B]Q: How do I report a bug?[/B]
A: GitHub issue with server version, LeafRTP version, platform, relevant config files, and the error log section. See the [URL='https://dailystruggle.github.io/RTP/FOR_SERVER_ADMINS/']admin guide[/URL] for the full reproduction template.

[/SPOILER]

[SPOILER="Community Support Policy (read before filing an issue)"]

Support for the free build is [B]community-tier and best-effort[/B]. A solo maintainer ships fixes when properly-reported issues land.

[LIST]
[*]Support covers bugs and configuration questions, after you've read the admin guide.
[*]Bug reports need a reproduction: server version, LeafRTP version, platform (Spigot / Paper / fork), [I]config.yml[/I], [I]regions/[/I], [I]safety.yml[/I], and the relevant [I]server.log[/I] section. Reports without these are asked for them once, then closed.
[*]"It doesn't work" is not a bug report. Tell me what you did, what you expected, and what actually happened.
[*]Response time: no SLA on the free build. Critical safety issues jump the queue regardless.
[*]Feature requests via GitHub issues. Priority follows the published roadmap, not ticket volume.
[/LIST]

[/SPOILER]

